  "topic_en": "This spread finishes Euler's worked example for the divisor 311 (minimal form 2^155 − 1) and works a second example for 233 (2^29 − 1); it then begins a table, for primes up to 400, of the simplest form 2^x ± 1 each prime divides.",
  "transcription": "256\n\n$+23-311=-288=+2^5.9$ [?] . . . . 126\n$+9+311=+320=+2^6.5$ . . . . 132\n$+5+311=+316=+2^2.79$ . . . . 134\n$+79-311=-232=-2^3.29$ . . . . 137\n$-29-311=-340=-2^2.85$ . . . . 139\n$-85-311=-396=-2^2.99$ . . . . 141\n$-99+311=+212=+2^2.53$ . . . . 143\n$+53+311=+364=+2^2.91$ . . . . 145\n$+91-311=-220=-2^2.55$ . . . . 147\n$-55+311=+256=2^8.1$ . . . . 155\nergo $x=155$, sicque minima formula, per 311 diuisibilis est $2^{155}-1$.\n\nSi substituissemus in $25^{ta}$ operatione, habuissemus $2^{x-75}$, eiusque residuum 47; et sumtis quadratis $2^{2x-150}$, siue per principalem diuidendo, $2^{x-150}$ cum residuo 2209, siue $32=2^5.1$, vnde, potestas $2^{x-155}$ residuum optatum producit $+1$. Sin autem in operatione $17^{ma}$ substituissemus, habuissemus $2^{x-50}$ cum residuo 7; sumtisque cubis $2^{3x-150}$ cum residuo 343, siue $32=2^5.1$, ita vt iam potestas $2^{3x-155}$, siue etiam $2^{x-155}$ residuum det $+1$; vnde sequitur $x=155$, vt ante.\n\nExemplum II.\n\n§. 23. Sit diuisor $N=233$, et sumto primo residuo $=1$, faciemus: Summa exponent.\n$1-233=-232=-2^3.29$ . . . . 3\n$-29+233=+204=+2^2.51$ . . . . 5\n$+51+233=+284=+2^2.71$ . . . . 7\n$+71+233=+304=+2^4.19$ . . . . 11\n\n257\n\n$+19+233=+252=+2^2.63$ . . . . 13\n$+63+233=+296=+2^3.37$ . . . . 16\n$+37-233=-196=-2^2.49$ . . . . 18\n$-49+233=+184=+2^3.23$ . . . . 21\n$+23+233=+256=+2^8.1$ . . . . 29\n\nScholion.\n\n§. 24. Hac igitur methodo pro quolibet diuisore N facile computatur formula simplicissima $2^x\\pm1$ per eum diuisibilis. Haud igitur abs re visum est, tabulam hic adiungere, in qua pro omnibus numeris primis vsque ad 400 simplicissimae formulae exhibentur; diuisores autem primos commode in quatuor ordines, secundum formas $8n+1$; $8n-1$; $8n+3$ et $8n-3$, distribui conueniet:\n\nN $8n+1$ | $2^x\\pm1$ | N $8n-1$ | $2^x+1$\n1 | $2^0-1$ | 7 | $2^3-1$\n17 | $2^4+1$ | 23 | $2^{11}-1$\n41 | $2^{10}+1$ | 31 | $2^5-1$\n73 | $2^9-1$ | 47 | $2^{23}-1$\n89 | $2^{11}-1$ | 71 | $2^{35}-1$\n97 | $2^{24}+1$ | 79 | $2^{39}-1$\n113 | $2^{14}+1$ | 103 | $2^{51}-1$\n137 | $2^{34}+1$ | 127 | $2^7-1$\n193 | $2^{48}+1$ | 151 | $2^{15}-1$\n233 | $2^{29}-1$ [?] | 167 | $2^{83}-1$\n241 | $2^{12}+1$ | 191 | $2^{95}-1$\n257 | $2^8+1$ | 199 | $2^{99}-1$\n281 | $2^{35}+1$ | 223 | $2^{37}-1$\n\nEuleri Opusc. Anal. Tom. I. Kk",
